---
scope: .
description: >
  Canonical home of the AIDE methodology and the MCP server that delivers it — single source of truth for every downstream host project, with brain configuration owned by the user via a single editable file rather than by code-level backend registries. The brain config file's body carries six marker-bounded sections (orientation, config, playbook-index, study-playbook, update-playbook, research-index): the first two are read live by `aide_brain` (orientation = runtime briefing; config = backend-specific wiring flow), and the last four are install-time seeds whose bytes the integration's `config` body section prose consumes at first `/aide:brain config` to seed entry-point artifacts in the brain via the brain's own MCP write tools. `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]` — a null at any index marks an unwired slot the user must fill via `/aide:brain config`; per-integration body sections name which indexes mean what (e.g. for the bundled Obsidian default, `args[3]` is the absolute path to the user's brain root). The package never introspects which slot is which — that knowledge lives entirely in the per-integration prose. `cli/init` accepts exactly one optional argument — `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian` — that selects which bundled `brain.aide` template scaffolds; it writes the methodology layer plus the bundled `brain.aide` template (with YAML null at unwired slots) plus the `aide` MCP server entry, and it never writes a `.mcp.json` brain entry, never scaffolds entry-point artifacts, never creates the brain root directory, never asks the user for a brain path, token, or any other backend-specific value. Brain wiring is fully delegated to `/aide:brain config` on first `/aide` run, where `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` unconditionally re-seeds the bundled `brain.aide` if absent and `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` returns the integration-specific wiring prose the agent executes verbatim to fill the null slot(s), run sync, and seed the four entry-point artifacts into the brain. The `aide_brain` runtime tool accepts a `kind` parameter selecting which live section to return; install-time seed sections feed the integration prose's seeding step, not `aide_brain`. The shipped `/aide:brain` and `/aide:update-playbook` commands are thin routers that forward to the integration's prose. The package's code surface stays storage-agnostic; the bundled obsidian-flavored default legitimately ships connector-specific content inside the body section bytes because the bundled template IS the obsidian default and those bytes are user-owned the moment they land.
intent: >
  This repository is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology — Autonomous
  Intent-Driven Engineering, with a deliberate second reading as AI Domain
  Expert — and the MCP server that delivers it to agents in host projects.
  Intent is the primary driver: a `.aide` intent doc governs the code, and a
  research agent plays the role of the human domain expert an engineering
  team would otherwise need to hire. The repo has two jobs: author the
  canonical docs, and ship them through the MCP handshake and aide_init.
  Success is an agent in a brand-new host project receiving the full
  methodology via tooling alone, then working intent-first — describing what
  it wants in cascading layers of abstraction and letting the agent turn
  those layers into code. The brain layer — the external knowledge store
  the methodology routes agents through for cross-project knowledge — is
  configured per host project by a single user-editable file the host owns
  and edits directly, not by a registry of connector identities baked into
  the server's code. That single file carries both the agent-facing
  runtime briefing the brain interface tool returns live and the
  integration-specific wiring flow `/aide:brain config` executes verbatim,
  so per-backend orchestration is a host-side prose edit instead of a
  shipped-code change. The cold-install CLI accepts exactly one optional
  argument — `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian` — that
  selects which bundled `brain.aide` template scaffolds; it writes the
  methodology layer (docs, agents, commands, skills, framework-pointer
  stub), writes the bundled `brain.aide` template into
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` with YAML null at unwired slots, and writes
  the `aide` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json` — and it never writes a
  `.mcp.json` brain entry, never scaffolds the four entry-point
  artifacts, never creates the brain root directory, never asks the
  user for backend-specific values like absolute paths, API tokens, or
  connection URIs. The brain MCP entry and the entry-point artifacts
  are wired by `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run after
  install, where `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` unconditionally
  re-seeds the bundled `brain.aide` if absent (its other six install
  steps are gone — the four entry-point artifacts and the brain root
  directory are no longer scaffolded by any package code path), and the
  integration's own `config` body section drives the agent through
  filling the null slot(s), running sync, and seeding the four
  entry-point artifacts into the brain via the brain's own MCP write
  tools from the seed-section bytes in `brain.aide`. The brain
  template's `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]` — a
  YAML null at any index is the explicit unwired-slot signal,
  replacing every prior literal-string-placeholder design — and
  per-integration body sections name which arg indexes mean what; the
  package never introspects slot semantics. Shipped pipeline commands
  that touch the brain are thin routers that forward to that prose;
  integration-specific argument shapes, parameter lists, and edit
  recipes live next to the user, not in the package. Full autonomy
  through intent-driven development, with AI domain experts filling
  the expertise gap and brain wiring (including the per-backend wiring
  flow and the timing of when wiring happens) under direct user
  control, is the north star every other decision in this repo must
  serve.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      The canonical AIDE methodology — spec, template, progressive disclosure,
      agent-readable code, automated QA — lives in this repo as editable doc
      files and is the single source of truth. Editing those files is the
      only way to change what downstream host projects receive.
    - >-
      Two delivery channels, each with one clear job: MCP tool descriptions
      teach AIDE at handshake time; aide_init installs a short pointer stub
      into the host's config file, lands the progressively-disclosed
      methodology doc hub onto the host's disk, scaffolds pipeline slash
      commands, wires the MCP server into the host project, and configures
      IDE preview. Together they cover the full lifecycle from handshake
      to steady-state work. The stub-plus-hub shape of the methodology
      delivery is load-bearing: every session in the host pays the stub
      cost on every read of the config file, but pays the full doctrine
      cost only when the task actually touches `.aide` files. Progressive
      disclosure applies at the delivery layer, not just inside the docs.
    - >-
      Every downstream artifact the server ships — tool descriptions,
      host-installed pointer stubs, host-installed methodology docs,
      slash command templates — is composed from the canonical doc
      files at build or init time, never duplicated as an inline string
      or hardcoded template.
    - >-
      A host project that runs aide_init once is AIDE-ready: the installing
      agent did not have to paraphrase, remember, or quote any AIDE rules to
      get there, because the tooling carried the full methodology into the
      project.
    - >-
      The repo itself exemplifies AIDE because the server is written using
      it: progressive disclosure in the folder tree, agent-readable names,
      `.aide` specs next to orchestrators, decisions-not-descriptions in
      every spec. Cascading intent at every level, so a contributor can
      write a new submodule spec by inheritance without re-deriving the
      rules.
    - >-
      Brain configuration in every host project lives in a single
      user-editable file at the host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the
      canonical and only source of truth for the host's brain layer.
      Its frontmatter declares exactly two things: a human-readable
      `name` for the wired brain (the user and the agent read it as a
      label; the package never branches on it) and a `mcpServerConfig`
      object naming the launcher command and args (consumed by sync,
      written into `.mcp.json`). Every other agent-facing concern —
      which MCP tool to call for reads, searches, writes, navigation;
      where to start; how to follow links; which arguments
      `/aide:brain config` accepts; what edit-and-sync sequence the
      backend's wiring flow runs — lives in the hand-written body
      sections, owned per consumer (the orientation section, the
      config section, the playbook-index section, the study-playbook
      section, the update-playbook section, the research-index section
      — see the body-schema outcome below). The default scaffolded
      `brain.aide` makes no use of substitution: `mcpServerConfig.args`
      carries the path inline, byte-for-byte under user ownership. The
      parser still supports `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter
      fields inside `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want
      to DRY a value across positions, but that surface is not
      exercised by the default scaffold. The body sections are
      hand-written verbatim per backend — no templating, no rendering,
      no agent-facing substitution. Adding, replacing, or retargeting
      a brain is a host-side edit to one file followed by a sync,
      never a code change to this package.
    - >-
      "The body of `brain.aide` is parsed as exactly six named sections
      — `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` — delimited by paired
      HTML-comment markers that the parser recognizes case-sensitively
      and lowercase-only. The recognized markers, in required order,
      are the `<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->` pair, the
      `<!-- aide-config-start -->` / `<!-- aide-config-end -->` pair,
      the `<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->` pair, the
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair, the
      `<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->` pair, and the
      `<!-- aide-research-index-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-research-index-end -->` pair — twelve markers total,
      six pairs, each section opened and closed by its own distinct
      tokens. The first two pairs (`orientation`, `config`) are live
      sections read by `aide_brain` at runtime; the last four pairs
      (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
      `researchIndex`) are install-time seeds whose bytes the
      integration's `config` body section prose consumes at first
      `/aide:brain config` to seed entry-point artifacts in the brain
      via the brain's own MCP write tools — `provisionBrain` itself
      never reads or writes those four sections, the package's code
      path stops at scaffolding the bundled `brain.aide` template.
      The live-vs-seed split is a structural property of the schema —
      `aide_brain` reads only the live pair and the integration prose
      reads only the seed pairs at first `/aide:brain config`, with
      per-consumer ownership enforced by the parser handing each
      consumer its own typed keys. Kebab-case marker tokens populate
      camelCase parsed field names (`playbook-index` populates
      `playbookIndex`, `study-playbook` populates `studyPlaybook`,
      `update-playbook` populates `updatePlaybook`, `research-index`
      populates `researchIndex`); no `Hub` suffix anywhere. The
      `Hub`-suffix shape is a retired regression class that extends
      across every seed-section field (`playbookIndexHub`,
      `studyPlaybookHub`, `updatePlaybookHub`, `researchIndexHub` are
      all rejected with the same reasoning that retired `playbookHub`
      / `researchHub`). The convention extends the existing
      `writeMethodology` HTML-comment marker precedent (same lowercase
      shape, distinct open/close per section). Marker layout
      violations are hard failures returning `malformed-body` naming
      the violating marker — missing pair, malformed or typo'd
      marker, unmatched close without prior open, open without close,
      wrong section order, nested markers all halt the pipeline.
      Bytes outside any marked section — between frontmatter and the
      first opener, between any closer and the next opener, after the
      last closer — are silently ignored by the parser; the parser's
      job is to extract the six named sections, not to police the
      rest of the file, so users may keep notes-to-self, scratch text,
      or comments in those regions without warning or error.
      Pre-pivot `brain.aide` files (old heading-based body schema
      with `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`),
      pre-amendment three-section marker-bounded files (prose /
      playbook / research only), four-section files using the prior
      `aide-prose-*` / `aide-playbook-*` / `aide-study-playbook-*` /
      `aide-research-*` markers, and any other arrangement that does
      not present all six new marker pairs in required order all
      return `malformed-body` naming the missing markers
      specifically — there is no transitional read path, no
      auto-rename rewrite, and no `aide_upgrade` carve-out that
      auto-injects missing pairs; users hand-edit, consistent with
      the existing strict-failure migration invariant for body
      schema changes."
    - >-
      "The framework's brain contract on cold install is exactly four
      required entry-point artifacts seeded from install-time seed body
      sections of `brain.aide`: the coding-playbook entry-point artifact
      (sourced from the `playbookIndex` section), the study-playbook
      entry-point artifact (sourced from the `studyPlaybook` section,
      holding the backend-specific playbook-navigation methodology the
      `study-playbook` skill points at), the update-playbook entry-point
      artifact (sourced from the `updatePlaybook` section, holding the
      backend-specific playbook-maintenance methodology the
      `/aide:update-playbook` shipped command points at), and the
      research entry-point artifact (sourced from the `researchIndex`
      section). Seeding is done by the integration's `aide-config` body
      section prose at first `/aide:brain config` — after the user
      fills the unwired slot(s) and runs sync — by reading the
      seed-section bytes from `brain.aide` via `parseBrainAide`'s typed
      keys and writing each artifact into the brain via the brain's
      own MCP write tool (e.g. `mcp__brain__write_note` for the
      bundled Obsidian default) at paths the integration's prose
      names. `provisionBrain` itself never writes these artifacts —
      its code path stops at scaffolding the bundled `brain.aide`
      template plus planning the `.mcp.json` brain entry; everything
      downstream of that lives in user-owned prose. For an
      editor-backed Markdown brain — the bundled default's storage
      shape — those artifacts land at
      `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
      `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
      `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and `research/research.md`
      respectively; how they manifest in any other storage is the
      user's `brain.aide` concern, not the package's. Every artifact's
      bytes flow from a `brain.aide` body section through the parser,
      never from an inline TypeScript constant in the install module.
      Once the integration prose has seeded an entry-point artifact
      into the brain, the brain owns it: subsequent edits happen in
      the brain file, not in `brain.aide`'s seed section, and no
      shipped code path re-reads or re-overwrites the on-disk
      artifact on later runs. The agent-side `study-playbook` skill
      and the shipped `/aide:update-playbook` command both stay
      minimal: each is a thin pointer at its respective on-disk
      entry-point artifact, and the navigation/maintenance methodology
      travels with the user-owned brain template, not with the
      package's release cycle."
    - >-
      "Shipped pipeline commands that touch the brain are thin routers
      that forward to the integration's user-owned prose; per-backend
      argument shapes, parameter lists, and edit recipes live in
      `brain.aide` body sections, never in the shipped command files.
      `/aide:brain config` is a fixed-shape router that calls
      `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })` UNCONDITIONALLY at the top
      of every invocation — the call is not gated on
      `aide_info.brain.status`, not branched on `no-brain-aide`, not
      skipped when a `brain.aide` already exists. The unconditional
      call is what makes a missing `brain.aide` self-heal:
      `provisionBrain`'s presence-only idempotency re-scaffolds the
      bundled template in place when absent, without touching an
      existing one. Missing entry-point artifact recovery is a
      separate self-heal loop owned by the integration's `aide-config`
      body section prose: the prose's seeding step runs every
      `/aide:brain config`, presence-checks each of the four entry-
      point artifacts in the brain, and re-seeds any that are missing
      from the seed-section bytes — `provisionBrain` plays no part
      in entry-point recovery. After the unconditional `aide_init`
      call, the router pulls integration-specific prose via
      `aide_brain({ kind: \"config\" })` and executes the returned
      instructions verbatim, forwarding any user `$ARGUMENTS`
      opaquely to the integration's prose. The shipped command never
      parses `$ARGUMENTS`, never branches on backend identity, never
      hardcodes a backend's argument shape (path, token, JSON blob,
      or otherwise) — the integration's `config` body section is the
      only place that documents what users can put after `/aide:brain
      config` and what the named arg indexes mean. The brain command
      surface is minimal: only `config`. There is no `doctor`
      subcommand, no `repair` subcommand, no `status` subcommand —
      missing-file recovery for `brain.aide` is folded into
      `config`'s unconditional `aide_init` call and missing
      entry-point artifact recovery is folded into the integration
      prose's presence-check + re-seed loop, neither exposed as a
      separate verb. `/aide:update-playbook` is a one-step router: it
      calls `aide_brain` for the brain's read tool, reads the
      on-disk update-playbook entry-point artifact, and follows the
      maintenance methodology that artifact describes. Both commands
      are essentially framing-plus-pointer prose; the substance lives
      in the user-owned brain content. This is the same shape the
      `study-playbook` skill already follows."
    - >-
      `brain.aide` is a documented AIDE file type sitting alongside
      `intent.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide`, and `research.aide` in
      the canonical methodology — with its own dedicated methodology doc
      under `.aide/docs/` describing its schema, lifecycle, and the
      explicit divergence that it is a config file, not an intent spec
      (no `scope`, no `outcomes`, its frontmatter is a typed config
      object and its body is hand-written agent-facing prose). The
      methodology doc ships from this repo to every host project the
      same way every other canonical doc does.
    - >-
      "The MCP server never dispatches on connector identity. `aide_brain`
      reads the host's `brain.aide` and returns one of its live body
      sections verbatim alongside a status — no server-side substitution
      at the section layer, no rendering pass, no template engine. The
      tool accepts a single optional `kind` parameter selecting which
      live section to return: `kind: \"orientation\"` (the default;
      runtime briefing — what the brain is, which tools to call to
      read it, where the entry-point artifacts live) and
      `kind: \"config\"` (the integration-specific wiring flow
      `/aide:brain config` executes verbatim). Only the two live
      sections are reachable via `aide_brain`; install-time seed
      sections (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
      `researchIndex`) are never returned by `aide_brain` because
      after install their authoritative bytes live in the brain itself
      as on-disk entry-point artifacts, not in the seed sections. The
      response shape carries status and the requested section's bytes
      only — no `backend` field, no connector label, no enum the
      server interprets. What brain is wired is information for the
      user and the agent reading the literal prose, not for the
      server's control flow. Different connectors ship structurally
      different prose across both live sections; sharing prose across
      connectors via templating would force a uniformity their actual
      instructions do not have."
    - >-
      "The cold-install CLI accepts exactly one optional argument —
      `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian` — and no other
      arguments of any kind, ever. `npx aidemd-mcp init` and `npx
      aidemd-mcp init --brain obsidian` are the bundled-default cold
      starts; future integrations land their own bundled templates
      and become valid `--brain` values, but the user never supplies
      backend-specific values like absolute paths, API tokens, or
      connection URIs to `cli/init`. `cli/init` writes the methodology
      layer (methodology docs, pipeline agent files, slash commands,
      skills, the framework pointer stub, and the IDE preview wiring),
      writes the bundled `brain.aide` template selected by `--brain
      <integration>` into `.aide/config/brain.aide` — emitting YAML
      null at every unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args` — and
      writes the `aide` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json`'s
      `mcpServers[\"aide\"]`. It does NOT write a `.mcp.json` entry
      under `mcpServers[\"brain\"]`. It does NOT scaffold any of the
      four entry-point artifacts. It does NOT create the brain root
      directory. It does NOT call any seeding code path or ask the
      user for a brain path, token, or connection URI at install
      time. Brain MCP entry wiring and entry-point artifact seeding
      are fully delegated to `/aide:brain config` on the first
      `/aide` run after install, and the cold install's terminal
      warning routes the user there explicitly. `cli/init` accepts
      no `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`,
      `--brain-token`, `--brain-name`, `--brain-url`, or any other
      brain-shaped flag in the current shape — the only sanctioned
      brain-shaped argument on the install CLI is `--brain
      <integration>` selecting which bundled template scaffolds, and
      that argument never collects a backend-specific value."
    - >-
      `.aide/config/` is the canonical home of user-owned configuration
      in every host project. Files inside are scaffolded once at
      `aide_init` if absent, then belong to the user forever — neither
      `aide_init` nor `aide_upgrade` overwrites, patches, or migrates
      anything under `.aide/config/` on subsequent runs. Files outside
      `.aide/config/` (the methodology doc hub at `.aide/docs/`,
      pipeline command templates, agent definitions, skills, every
      other artifact the package ships) are package-owned and
      re-installed on every init/upgrade. The boundary is the directory
      path itself — install/upgrade tooling reads the path as the
      ownership signal, with no per-file allowlist. `brain.aide` is the
      current inhabitant; the convention is forward-compatible with
      future user-owned config files.
    - >-
      "`brain.aide` carries no `rootPath` field — the brain's location
      lives exactly once, inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`. The package
      never derives a logical root from args, never stats a path to
      validate it, never introspects args to infer connector identity,
      never inspects which arg index means what. Path validation is
      the launcher's job at MCP server startup; the package does not
      second-guess it. Agents call MCP tools with vault-relative paths
      and trust the server's responses. Boot reporter brain status
      states stay reduced to `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`,
      and `mcp-drift` — no fifth state is added for the unwired-slot
      case. `buildBrainState` maps any null entry in `mcpServerConfig.args`
      to `no-mcp-entry` semantically: a null-bearing config is by
      definition not a wireable launch command, so the brain's MCP
      entry is treated as not-yet-wired regardless of whether
      `.mcp.json` happens to contain a stale entry from a prior
      session. The four-state vocabulary is closed; null-armed configs
      collapse into the existing `no-mcp-entry` state rather than
      forking it."
    - >-
      "`brain.aide`'s frontmatter contains exactly two top-level fields:
      `name` (string, the human-readable label for the wired brain) and
      `mcpServerConfig` (object with `command` and `args`, consumed by
      sync to write the `.mcp.json` brain entry). No `connector`, no
      `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` — the schema is the
      minimum that still has real consumers. `name` is a label; the
      package never branches on it. Different brains may share names
      without affecting code behavior. The label exists for the human
      reading the file and for the agent narrating, in conversation
      with the user, which brain it is connected to.
      `mcpServerConfig.command` is a string. `mcpServerConfig.args` is
      typed `(string | null)[]` — every element is either a fully-formed
      argument string or YAML null (a bare `-` with no scalar after,
      which YAML parses to `null`). A null at any index is the explicit
      unwired-slot signal: it means \"the user has not yet supplied the
      value this slot needs.\" Sync writes the post-interpolation
      `mcpServerConfig` byte-for-byte into `.mcp.json`'s
      `mcpServers[\"brain\"]` entry; if any arg is null at sync time,
      sync refuses (see the sync-refusal outcome below). No literal
      string sentinel — `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `${...}`-shaped
      placeholder, magic UUID, or any other in-band marker — is part of
      the design. YAML null is the contract."
    - >-
      "Per-integration ownership of arg-index slot semantics. The
      meaning of each slot in `mcpServerConfig.args` — which index is
      a path, which is a token, which is a connection URI, which is
      already fully wired by the launcher itself — is documented
      exclusively inside that integration's `config` body section in
      its bundled `brain.aide` template. The bundled Obsidian default
      names `args[3]` as the absolute path to the user's brain root;
      a future Mem0 integration would name `args[2]` as the API token
      and `args[3]` as the user identifier in its own template's
      `config` section; whatever future integrations exist, each
      describes its own slot map in its own prose. The shipped
      `/aide:brain config` router NEVER introspects which slot is
      which — it forwards `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely and lets the
      integration's prose drive every per-slot decision. There is no
      shipped registry of slot meanings, no per-index validation
      table, no `argSlots` frontmatter field declaring what each index
      is supposed to be. Slot semantics live in the user-owned body,
      same as every other per-backend concern. This is what makes
      adding a new backend a host-side prose addition rather than a
      package code change."
    - >-
      The brain's interface contract is the body sections, not a
      frontmatter declaration. There is no frontmatter field declaring
      expected MCP tool names, expected operations (read, search, write,
      list), or expected entry-point paths — the schema does not
      enumerate any of those. The agent reads the relevant body section
      verbatim, calls whatever MCP tools the section names, and
      surfaces failures (missing tool, wrong shape, unwired backend)
      to the user at the first call. Failure is fast, specific, and
      unmistakable: a user whose MCP session does not expose what
      their body section names finds out immediately, not after
      passing a parser check that lied. Placing the binding cost in
      one place — the body sections — instead of forcing the user to
      keep frontmatter declarations and section content in sync is
      the whole point. The schema's minimum justifies its maximum:
      every frontmatter field must name a real runtime consumer (sync
      writes `mcpServerConfig` into `.mcp.json`; the user and agent
      read `name` as a label). A frontmatter field with no code path
      that consumes its value is validation theater and is rejected.
    - >-
      "`brain.aide` and the host's `.mcp.json` brain entry stay in sync
      through one explicit, user-driven action — `npx aidemd-mcp sync` —
      that reads `brain.aide` and writes the corresponding `.mcp.json`
      brain entry. Sync is idempotent. Sync refuses to write
      null-bearing args: if any element of `mcpServerConfig.args` is
      null at sync time, sync exits non-zero, names the offending
      index(es), and routes the user to `/aide:brain config` to fill
      them. Sync's contract is byte-faithful propagation of
      `mcpServerConfig` from `brain.aide` into `.mcp.json` — but
      propagating null into a launch command is propagating broken
      state, not a useful byte-faithful action. The MCP server never
      silently rewrites `.mcp.json`; mutation of the host's MCP wiring
      is always a deliberate user action with a visible command
      boundary."
    - >-
      Drift between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` is a hard halt for the
      pipeline. The boot reporter detects drift and surfaces it as the
      `mcp-drift` brain status state, which the orchestrator treats the
      same way it treats a missing MCP entry — the pipeline does not
      proceed until the user runs sync. The brain status state set is
      `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` — covering
      the healthy case, the absence of `brain.aide`, the absence of the
      `.mcp.json` brain entry, and the drift case respectively.
    - >-
      "Storage-agnostic vocabulary across every surface this package
      authors. The forbidden-token list — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note`
      (used as a synonym for \"file\"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and
      `hub` — must not appear in any user-facing prose surface (MCP
      tool descriptions, the README, the canonical methodology docs
      under `.aide/docs/`, slash-command prompts under
      `.claude/commands/`, agent definitions under `.claude/agents/`,
      and every `.aide` spec authored in this repo) nor in code
      identifiers, comments, or string constants project-wide. The
      brain layer is described to agents and users in storage-agnostic
      terms — \"brain\", \"brain root\", \"the brain's orientation
      section\", \"the config section\", \"the playbook-index
      section\", \"the study-playbook section\", \"the update-playbook
      section\", \"the research-index section\" — never in the
      vocabulary of any one connector. Three exceptions are explicitly
      allowed because each names a legitimate non-branching anchor
      rather than a code or prose decision: the
      `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name (describes
      bundled template content, not a dispatch axis), the `.obsidian`
      directory heuristic comment in `brainRootExists` (an
      honestly-labeled implementation detail in a single helper), and
      the body section bytes of the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`
      helper itself across all six named sections (`orientation`, `config`,
      `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
      `researchIndex`) — those bytes ship obsidian-flavored content
      because the bundled template IS the obsidian default, they
      become user-owned the moment they are scaffolded onto disk (for
      the live sections) or seeded into the brain (for the seed
      sections), and any host that wires a different brain edits the
      section bytes in their own `brain.aide`. Additionally, the
      integration name `obsidian` is allowed as a value of the
      `cli/init --brain <integration>` argument — a user-facing
      argument value that selects which bundled template scaffolds
      and never reaches a code dispatch axis. The
      bundled-template-body-bytes carve-out is scoped exactly: it
      covers the bytes inside the template's body section literals
      only — NOT the JSDoc, identifiers, parameter names, or function
      names in the template module's `index.ts`, NOT string constants
      anywhere outside the body section literals, NOT anti-example
      bullets in undesired-outcome blocks (those have their own
      anti-example carve-out below), and NOT any other bundled
      template should one ever exist unless it is explicitly named as
      another connector-flavored default. Anti-example occurrences
      inside undesired-outcome blocks of specs are also allowed —
      naming a forbidden token to forbid it is the mechanism, not a
      violation. The safe-list of storage-agnostic body-field names
      is exactly `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`; the
      `Hub`-suffix regression class extends across every seed-section
      field (`playbookIndexHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`,
      `updatePlaybookHub`, `researchIndexHub` are all rejected with
      the same reasoning that retired `playbookHub` / `researchHub`).
      Every other appearance of the forbidden tokens, in code or
      prose, is a regression."
    - >-
      The in-code backend registry — matchers, drivers, identity inference
      — is fully retired. There is no module in this package whose job is
      to enumerate connectors, infer connector identity from MCP entries,
      or dispatch behavior on a connector enum. Adding a new brain is a
      user-side edit, never a code-level extension to this package.
  undesired:
    - >-
      A rule, convention, or doctrine that lives anywhere other than the
      canonical doc files — a commit message, a README, a code comment, a
      per-project CLAUDE.md edit. If the change is not in the canonical
      docs, it is not shipped, and it will drift away from whatever the
      server actually delivers.
    - >-
      Dual sources of truth: the same doctrine appearing in both this repo
      and the original brain notes without one being marked authoritative
      and the other deleted. Migration from the brain is a one-way move on
      the same commit.
    - >-
      A delivery channel other than MCP tool descriptions and aide_init —
      a sixth tool that "also explains things," a server-side prompt, a
      standalone CLI that teaches. Every additional channel fragments the
      teaching surface and forces agents to guess where to look.
    - >-
      A canonical doc that describes behavior the server does not actually
      deliver. Aspirational docs are worse than missing docs, because
      missing docs do not lie.
    - >-
      A change that makes the cascading-intent model less faithful, less
      complete, or less teachable — a child spec restating its parent, a
      parent so narrow that its children have nothing to inherit, a helper
      with a `.aide` file, an orchestrator without one. These are
      regressions even when they add features.
    - >-
      Any code path in this package that branches on connector identity —
      a switch on `connector`, a registry lookup keyed by name, a matcher
      that maps an MCP entry to a "known backend" so dispatch can pick a
      driver. The server reads `brain.aide` and returns its prose body
      verbatim; it does not know nor care what brand of brain is wired.
    - >-
      A `brain.aide` whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
      conventions — a `scope` field, an `intent` paragraph, an `outcomes`
      block, a `status` lifecycle field. `brain.aide` is a config file,
      not an intent spec; conflating the two erodes the distinction
      between intent contracts and runtime configuration and confuses
      every downstream agent that has to decide which rules apply.
    - >-
      The MCP server silently rewriting the host's `.mcp.json` to "fix"
      drift, "auto-sync" on boot, or repair a missing brain entry. Every
      mutation of the host's MCP wiring must be an explicit user action
      run through the sync verb; auto-repair shifts a load-bearing
      contract surface out from under the user.
    - >-
      `aide_init` or `aide_upgrade` overwriting, patching, migrating, or
      otherwise mutating any file under `.aide/config/` on a subsequent
      run. Files in that directory are user-owned the moment they are
      scaffolded; any code path that touches them after their first
      write — even to "fix" a stale template, land newer defaults, or
      tidy formatting — shifts a load-bearing user surface out from
      under the user. Same class of failure as the MCP server silently
      rewriting `.mcp.json`. New defaults reach existing hosts only
      through the sync verb or a deliberate user edit, never through
      the install or upgrade path.
    - >-
      Any code path that introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract
      a vault path, infer connector identity, or stat a derived path.
      Path validation is the launcher's job at startup; the package
      does not second-guess it. Reintroducing a `rootPath` field, an
      `invalid-path` boot state, or any per-connector path validation
      is the same regression in different clothing — the brain
      refactor's no-connector-branching invariant must hold even
      against helpers that look like generic plumbing.
    - >-
      Any frontmatter field added to `brain.aide` whose only consumer
      is the parser's own validation — `tools.*` declaring expected
      MCP tool names the runtime never actually checks; `entryFile`
      naming a path no code reads; `connector` declaring a backend
      identifier the server is forbidden from branching on; `rootPath`
      shadowing a value already inline in args. This is validation
      theater: a field that exists to nag the user into filling it in,
      with no code path that consumes the value once filled. The
      schema must justify each field by naming a real runtime consumer
      (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name`
      as a label). Anything else belongs in the prose body where the
      agent actually reads it, or nowhere at all.
    - >-
      Connector-specific prose hardcoded into the package — Obsidian
      launcher snippets baked into `aide_brain`'s response, Notion-shaped
      examples compiled into the rendered output, vault-path conventions
      assumed in the boot reporter. The only place connector-specific
      content lives is inside `brain.aide` itself, where the user owns
      and edits it.
    - >-
      "Server-side substitution applied to any of the six body sections
      — placeholder rendering, template engines, variable interpolation,
      anything that mutates a section's bytes between disk and the
      consumer (`aide_brain`'s response for the live sections, the
      integration prose's seeding step for the seed sections). All
      six sections are hand-written per connector and ship verbatim;
      introducing a substitution pass on any of them conflates the
      install-time interpolation surface (`mcpServerConfig.args`) with
      the section-content surface and forces every section author to
      think about which strings will be rewritten. Substitution lives
      only in `mcpServerConfig.args`, never in any of the
      `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `updatePlaybook`, or `researchIndex` body sections."
    - >-
      Boot reporting that reduces the post-refactor brain layer to a
      single binary state — wired or not. Without explicit states for
      "no `brain.aide`" and "drift between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json`,"
      the pipeline cannot tell the user what to do to recover, and the
      orchestrator cannot enforce the hard halt the sync contract
      depends on.
    - >-
      "A shipped pipeline command file under `.claude/commands/` that
      hardcodes integration-specific argument shape, parameter
      vocabulary, or backend-specific edit recipes — `/aide:brain
      config` parsing `$ARGUMENTS` as an absolute path because Obsidian
      happens to want a path; `/aide:update-playbook` calling
      `mcp__brain__patch_note` by name because Obsidian happens to use
      Markdown patches; either command branching on the brain's
      `name` label or sniffing `mcpServerConfig.args` to decide what
      to do. Shipped commands are thin routers; integration-specific
      orchestration belongs in the user-owned `brain.aide` body
      sections (the `config` section for runtime flow, the
      `update-playbook` seed section for the maintenance methodology
      that lands in the brain). A command that knows what kind of
      brain it's talking to is the in-code backend registry coming
      back through the command surface."
    - >-
      "An `aide_brain` `kind` parameter value that exposes one of the
      install-time seed sections — `kind: \"playbook-index\"`,
      `kind: \"study-playbook\"`, `kind: \"update-playbook\"`,
      `kind: \"research-index\"`, or any future seed section — at
      runtime. Live sections (`orientation`, `config`) are read by
      `aide_brain`; seed sections become on-disk entry-point artifacts
      in the brain after install and are read from there, not from
      `brain.aide`. Letting `aide_brain` return a seed section creates
      a second authoritative source for the same content (the
      brain.aide section AND the on-disk artifact) and lies to the
      agent about which one its edits should target. The schema's
      live-vs-seed split is enforceable at the tool surface or it is
      not enforced at all."
    - >-
      "Any transitional read path or `aide_upgrade` carve-out that
      auto-rewrites pre-rework `brain.aide` files (renaming
      `aide-prose-*` to `aide-orientation-*`, renaming
      `aide-playbook-*` to `aide-playbook-index-*`, renaming
      `aide-research-*` to `aide-research-index-*`, auto-injecting
      missing `aide-config-*` or `aide-update-playbook-*` pairs).
      `brain.aide` is user-owned the moment it lands; mutating it
      from any code path on a subsequent run shifts a load-bearing
      user surface out from under the user — the same regression
      class as `aide_init`/`aide_upgrade` rewriting `.aide/config/`
      files or the MCP server silently rewriting `.mcp.json`. Strict
      `malformed-body` failure that names every missing marker and
      forces the user to hand-edit is the only acceptable migration
      path, consistent with the prior 3→4 amendment."
    - >-
      "A literal-string sentinel anywhere in the unwired-slot design —
      `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `<VAULT_ROOT>`, `__UNWIRED__`, a
      magic UUID, or any other in-band string the package recognizes
      as \"this is empty, please fill me in.\" The retired
      `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder is the canonical example: it produced
      a configuration where `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` agreed
      byte-for-byte (both contained the literal `<BRAIN_PATH>`), the
      drift detector returned `ok`, the orchestrator never routed to
      `/aide:brain config`, and the user discovered the broken state
      only when the brain MCP server failed to launch. YAML null is
      the unwired-slot signal — a structural property of the config,
      not a string the package has to recognize. Any code path,
      template, or doc that brings back a literal sentinel under any
      name is the same regression class."
    - >-
      "`cli/init` writing any brain surface beyond
      `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the selected bundled template, or
      accepting any brain-shaped argument other than `--brain
      <integration>`. Writing a `.mcp.json` entry under
      `mcpServers[\"brain\"]`, scaffolding any of the four entry-point
      artifacts, creating the brain root directory, or asking the user
      for a brain path / API token / connection URI / brain name /
      brain URL at install time are all the same regression: brain
      MCP entry wiring and entry-point seeding belong to the first
      `/aide` run's `/aide:brain config` step, not to the cold
      install. A `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `--brain-root`,
      `--brain-token`, `--brain-name`, `--brain-url`, or any other
      brain-shaped flag on the install CLI other than `--brain
      <integration>` is forbidden. The single sanctioned brain-shaped
      argument — `--brain <integration>`, defaulting to `obsidian` —
      selects WHICH bundled template `cli/init` scaffolds into
      `.aide/config/brain.aide`; it does NOT collect a
      backend-specific value, and it does NOT change the
      no-`.mcp.json`-brain-entry / no-entry-point-artifacts /
      no-brain-root-directory / no-backend-prompt invariants
      above."
    - >-
      "`cli/sync` writing null-bearing `mcpServerConfig.args` into
      `.mcp.json`. Sync's contract is byte-faithful propagation of
      `mcpServerConfig` — but propagating null into a launch command
      is propagating broken state, not byte-faithful action. Sync
      must hard-fail on any null arg: exit non-zero, name the
      offending index(es), and route the user to `/aide:brain
      config`. A sync that silently coerces null to empty string,
      drops the null entry, substitutes a placeholder, or otherwise
      converts the unwired-slot signal into a string in the launch
      command is the regression that produced the original
      `<BRAIN_PATH>`-passes-the-drift-check bug in different clothing."
    - >-
      "A `/aide:brain config` router that conditionally calls
      `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })` based on
      `aide_info.brain.status` (skipping the call when the status is
      anything other than `no-brain-aide`, or when `brain.aide`
      already exists, or when entry-point seed artifacts already
      exist on disk). The unconditional call is what makes
      missing-`brain.aide` recovery self-heal: a user whose prior
      install was interrupted or whose template version changed
      gets the bundled template re-scaffolded in place by
      `provisionBrain`'s presence-only idempotency on every
      `/aide:brain config` invocation. (Missing entry-point artifact
      recovery is owned by the integration prose's presence-check +
      re-seed loop, not by `provisionBrain`.) A conditional call
      pushes recovery into a separate `doctor` / `repair` / `status`
      subcommand the agent has to know to invoke — the brain command
      surface stays minimal at one verb (`config`), and recovery
      folds into that verb's preamble."
    - >-
      "A boot-reporter or `buildBrainState` mapping that introduces
      a fifth status state for null-armed args — `unwired`,
      `incomplete-args`, `placeholder-present`, or any other label
      that splits the unwired-slot case off from `no-mcp-entry`.
      The four-state vocabulary (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`,
      `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) is closed; null-bearing configs
      collapse semantically into `no-mcp-entry` because a null arg
      means the brain's MCP entry is not yet wireable. Forking a
      fifth state would force every consumer of the four-state
      union — orchestrator routing, the `/aide` flow, the boot
      reporter — to grow a fifth branch with no different remedy
      than `no-mcp-entry` already has (route to `/aide:brain
      config`)."
    - >-
      "A shipped command file, code path, or registry that knows
      which arg index in `mcpServerConfig.args` means what — a
      `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constant or any other named integer
      hardcoding a slot position, a `resolveBrainRoot`-style helper
      that reads `mcpServerConfig.args[N]` to extract a path or any
      other slot semantic, a switch on `args[3]` because Obsidian's
      path lives there, a matcher recognizing token-shaped strings
      at `args[2]`, an `argSlots` frontmatter field declaring \"this
      index is a path,\" a `/aide:brain config` router that
      introspects which slots are null to decide what to ask the
      user for. Slot semantics live exclusively in the per-integration
      `config` body section — the package never inspects which slot
      is which. A command or code path that grows arg-index
      awareness is the in-code backend registry coming back through
      the slot surface."
    - >-
      "Any `provisionBrain` step that writes an entry-point artifact
      to disk, creates the brain root directory, or otherwise scaffolds
      anything beyond the bundled `brain.aide` template and the
      `.mcp.json` brain entry plan. `provisionBrain`'s pipeline is
      exactly two `InitStep`s — scaffold `.aide/config/brain.aide`
      from the selected bundled template (with YAML null at unwired
      slots), and plan the `.mcp.json` brain entry. The retired
      `buildPlaybookStep`, `buildStudyPlaybookStep`,
      `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`, `buildResearchStep`, and
      `buildBrainRootStep` step builders — and any successor under
      a renamed identifier carrying the same shape (a step that
      reads a seed body section and writes it to a path inside the
      brain, or a step that creates the brain root directory) —
      are gone. Entry-point artifacts are seeded by the
      integration's `aide-config` body section prose at first
      `/aide:brain config`, not by any package code path; the
      bytes flow from `brain.aide` seed sections via
      `parseBrainAide`'s typed keys (`playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and the
      writes happen via the brain's own MCP write tool. Restoring
      a step builder of either retired class — under any name — is
      the same regression."
---

## Context

This repository is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology — Autonomous
Intent-Driven Engineering, with a deliberate second reading as AI Domain
Expert — and the MCP server that delivers it to agents in host projects.
Intent is the primary driver: a `.aide` intent doc governs the code, and a
research agent fills the role of the domain expert an engineering team
would otherwise need to hire. The repo has two jobs: author the canonical
docs, and ship them through the MCP handshake and `aide_init`.

The MCP handshake is the only documentation surface a compliant MCP client
is required to present to the model verbatim, in every session, in every
project, without user action. `aide_init` is the only command needed to
turn a cold host project into one that knows AIDE. Together those two
channels give the methodology a delivery path that does not depend on the
agent remembering anything across sessions or the user editing any file
by hand. This repo's job is to author the methodology once, hold it as
the single source of truth, and ship it through those two channels.
Nothing else.

The brain layer — the external knowledge store the methodology routes
agents through for durable, cross-project knowledge — is configured per
host project by a single user-editable file at `.aide/config/brain.aide`.
The package has an opinion about how the host declares its brain, not
about which brain it runs against. The frontmatter declares exactly two
things — a human-readable `name` label and an `mcpServerConfig` object
with `command` and `args` — and the body carries six marker-bounded
named sections (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) each delimited by
its own paired HTML-comment markers. Adding a new brain is a host-side
edit to one file followed by a sync, never a code change to this
package.

The body's six sections split into two structural categories by who
reads them. Sections one and two — `orientation` and `config` — are
*live*: `aide_brain` reads them on demand whenever an agent or shipped
command needs them. Every read goes through the parser, and the user's
edits in `brain.aide` take effect on the next call. Sections three
through six — `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex` — are *seeds*: the install service reads them exactly
once on cold install and writes their bytes into files inside the
brain. After that the brain owns the on-disk file; the seed section
is dormant, and any later edit happens in the brain file, not in
`brain.aide`. The split is enforced at the consumer boundary:
`aide_brain`'s `kind` parameter is closed at two values
(`"orientation"` and `"config"`), and the install service reads the
four seed keys only. Cross-reading would re-create exactly the
dual-source-of-truth problem the architecture exists to prevent.

The framework's brain contract on cold install is exactly four
required entry-point artifacts — the coding-playbook index, the
study-playbook navigation guide, the update-playbook maintenance
guide, and the research index — each sourced from one seed body
section. For an editor-backed Markdown brain (the bundled default's
storage shape), those artifacts land at
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and `research/research.md`. How
they manifest in any other storage is the user's `brain.aide`
concern, not the package's. Every artifact's bytes flow from a body
section through the parser, never from a code-level constant. Once
the install service has scaffolded an artifact into the brain, the
brain owns it: subsequent edits happen in the brain file, not in the
seed section, and the package never re-reads or re-overwrites the
on-disk artifact on later runs.

Brain wiring is delegated to the first `/aide` run after install,
not done at install time. The cold-install CLI is argument-less and
brain-agnostic: `npx aidemd-mcp init` accepts no brain-shaped flag
of any kind. It installs the methodology layer only (methodology
docs, pipeline agent files, slash commands, skills, the framework
pointer stub, the IDE preview wiring) and touches no brain surface:
it does not write `.aide/config/brain.aide`, does not write a
`.mcp.json` brain entry, does not call the brain provisioner, does
not create the brain root directory, does not ask the user for any
backend-specific value. The cold install's terminal warning routes
the user to `/aide:brain config` for brain wiring, and that is the
surface where wiring happens.

`/aide:brain config` is a thin router that calls `aide_init({
category: "brain" })` unconditionally on every invocation. The call
is not gated on the brain's status, not branched on whether
`brain.aide` already exists, not skipped when entry-point files are
already on disk. The unconditional call is what makes missing-file
recovery self-heal: a user who deleted a seed artifact, whose prior
install was interrupted, or whose template version added a section
gets the missing artifacts re-seeded in place by the brain
provisioner's presence-only idempotency on every `/aide:brain
config` invocation. After the unconditional call, the router pulls
the integration's wiring prose via `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`
and executes the returned instructions verbatim, forwarding any
user `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely. The shipped command never parses
`$ARGUMENTS`, never branches on backend identity, never hardcodes a
backend's argument shape — the integration's `config` body section
is the only place that documents what users can put after
`/aide:brain config` and what the named arg indexes mean.

The unwired-slot signal is YAML null, not a string. The bundled
template ships `mcpServerConfig.args` typed `(string | null)[]` —
every element is either a fully-formed argument string or YAML
null (a bare `-` with no scalar after, which YAML parses to
`null`). A null at any index is the explicit, structural signal
that the user has not yet supplied the value the slot needs. The
package never introspects which slot is which: per-integration
body sections name the slot semantics in prose. The bundled
Obsidian default names `args[3]` as the absolute path to the user's
brain root in its `config` section; a future Mem0 integration would
name `args[2]` as the API token and `args[3]` as the user
identifier in its own template's `config` section. There is no
shipped registry of slot meanings, no per-index validation table,
no frontmatter field declaring what each index is supposed to be.
Slot semantics live in the user-owned body, same as every other
per-backend concern.

Sync is the only mutation surface for `.mcp.json`'s brain entry
in the entire package. `npx aidemd-mcp sync` reads `brain.aide`
and writes the post-interpolation `mcpServerConfig` byte-for-byte
into `mcpServers["brain"]`. Sync refuses to write null-bearing
args: if any element of `mcpServerConfig.args` is null at sync
time, sync exits non-zero, names the offending index(es), and
routes the user to `/aide:brain config` to fill them. Propagating
null into a launch command is propagating broken state, not a
useful byte-faithful action. The boot reporter detects drift
between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` and surfaces it as the
`mcp-drift` brain status state alongside `ok`, `no-brain-aide`,
and `no-mcp-entry` — a closed four-state vocabulary. Null-bearing
configs collapse semantically into `no-mcp-entry`: the brain
provisioner's `buildBrainState` maps any null entry in
`mcpServerConfig.args` to `no-mcp-entry`, since a null-bearing
config is by definition not a wireable launch command. No fifth
state is added for the unwired-slot case; the four-state
vocabulary is closed.

Two pipeline commands talk to the brain at runtime: `/aide:brain
config` and `/aide:update-playbook`. Both ship as thin routers —
files under `.claude/commands/` that contain framing prose, a
fixed sequence of tool calls, and zero integration-specific
knowledge. `/aide:update-playbook` calls `aide_brain` once for the
brain's read tool, reads `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` from
the brain, and follows the methodology that file describes.
Neither command knows whether the wired brain is Obsidian or
anything else; argument shape, parameter list, and edit recipe
live entirely in the user-owned prose — `brain.aide`'s `config`
section for runtime wiring, `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`
in the brain for maintenance methodology.

The package's code surface stays storage-agnostic across every
identifier, comment, JSDoc word, and string constant — but the
bundled obsidian-flavored default `brain.aide` template is, by
definition, the obsidian default, and its body section bytes ship
obsidian-flavored content across all six named sections. The
`orientation` section names the connector's MCP tools and points
at the four entry-point artifacts; the `config` section documents
the integration's argument shape and the wiring flow's
edit-and-sync sequence including which `args` index is the path
slot; the four seed sections seed artifacts shaped for an
editor-backed Markdown brain. Those bytes become user-owned the
moment they are scaffolded onto disk (for the live sections) or
seeded into the brain (for the seed sections): any host that wires
a different brain edits the relevant section bytes in their own
`brain.aide`, and the package's code surface stays storage-agnostic
regardless. The carve-out for the bundled template's body section
literals is the explicit boundary between agnostic code and
connector-flavored user-owned content.

## Strategy

**Canonical docs are editable files, not compiled output.** The doc set
lives in this repo as plain Markdown the maintainer can read, diff, and
edit. Every downstream artifact — tool descriptions, methodology blocks,
slash command templates — is composed from those files at build or init
time. A doctrine change is one edit in one file; propagation to every
downstream host project is automatic on their next init. If a maintainer
ever has to edit two places to ship one change, the source-of-truth
invariant has been broken and must be repaired, not worked around.

**Two delivery channels, no third.** MCP tool descriptions teach AIDE at
handshake time: what `.aide` files are, where they live, the naming rules,
the read-before-code workflow. `aide_init` installs the rest: a short
pointer stub into the host's config file, the full canonical methodology
doc set into a dedicated host-side doc hub the stub points at, the slash
commands that run the pipeline, the MCP wiring, and the IDE preview. The
two channels are cleanly partitioned — handshake teaches concepts, init
installs artifacts — and between them they cover the lifecycle an agent
in a host project actually walks. Any proposal to add a third channel
should be rejected and redirected into improving one of the existing two.

**Methodology delivery honors progressive disclosure at the delivery
layer.** The agent reads the host's config file on every session, so
that surface carries only a short stub that names the installed doc hub
and teaches the crawl trigger. The full canonical methodology lands as
files on the host's disk, read on demand by sessions that actually touch
`.aide` material. This mirrors what the user's global `study-playbook`
skill does against the brain — a shallow pointer surface naming a deep
content surface, with the agent crawling only when the task requires it.
Shipping the full methodology body inside the stub region violates the
pattern the methodology itself teaches, which is exactly the regression
this architecture exists to prevent.

**The repo eats its own dog food.** The MCP server is written using AIDE.
Progressive disclosure in the folder tree, folders named after their
default exports, JSDoc on every function, inline narration only on
orchestrators, `.aide` specs next to orchestrators and nowhere else,
decisions-not-descriptions in every spec. This is not stylistic — it is
the forcing function. A rule that is wrong or unworkable shows up as
pain in this repo before it ships to a host project. If a rule can't
survive being lived under here, it doesn't belong in the canonical docs.

**Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned.** The architectural
boundary is sharp: the server reads the host's `brain.aide`, parses
its frontmatter and its six marker-bounded body sections, and never
branches on connector identity. There is no module in this package
whose job is to enumerate brains, infer which one a host has wired, or
dispatch behavior on a connector enum. What replaces a registry is a
single file in the host's own repo: a typed two-field frontmatter the
host edits, and six hand-written body sections the host edits. Adding
a new brain is never a code change to this package; it is a host-side
edit plus a sync. The marker grammar follows the same lowercase shape
the existing methodology pointer-stub installer uses, extended with
distinct open and close tokens per section so a parser violation
names the offending marker by name rather than guessing where a
single-token region was supposed to start. The parser supports
`${...}` interpolation of frontmatter fields inside
`mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY a value
across argument positions; that surface remains for them but is not
exercised by the default scaffold. Substitution lives only in
`mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync time, applied by the
`.mcp.json` writer when sync runs — never in any of the six body
sections at agent time. All six body sections return verbatim, with
no rendering pass between disk and the consumer.

**Six body sections, each named for what it IS.** The schema names
field names that describe their consumer purpose: `orientation` (the
runtime briefing the agent reads on session start), `config` (the
integration-specific wiring flow `/aide:brain config` executes
verbatim), `playbookIndex` (the install-time *index* of the playbook,
distinct from the playbook content itself), `studyPlaybook` (the
install-time seed for the navigation methodology), `updatePlaybook`
(the install-time seed for the playbook-maintenance methodology),
and `researchIndex` (the install-time index of research). Each
section names a distinct consumer concern — runtime orientation,
runtime wiring, on-disk playbook index, on-disk navigation
methodology, on-disk maintenance methodology, on-disk research index.
Collapsing any two into one would force users to read past their
concern to find what they need. The kebab-case marker tokens populate
camelCase typed keys (`playbook-index → playbookIndex`,
`study-playbook → studyPlaybook`, `update-playbook → updatePlaybook`,
`research-index → researchIndex`), and the `Hub` suffix is a retired
regression class — `playbookIndexHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`,
`updatePlaybookHub`, `researchIndexHub` are all rejected.

**Live-vs-seed is enforced as a per-consumer ownership boundary at
the code level, not as a convention.** The first two sections
(`orientation`, `config`) are read live by `aide_brain` whenever an
agent or shipped command calls it; the last four (`playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) are read once at
cold install by the install service and written into the brain as
on-disk entry-point artifacts. After install the brain owns the
on-disk file; the seed section in `brain.aide` is dormant, and any
edit happens in the brain file. The split is enforced at the
consumer boundary: `aide_brain` MUST NOT read seed sections, and the
install service MUST NOT read live sections. Cross-reading would
re-create the dual-source-of-truth problem — if `aide_brain`
returned the `updatePlaybook` seed at runtime, the agent would have
two authoritative sources for the same content (the `brain.aide`
section AND the on-disk artifact in the brain), and edits made to
one would not propagate to the other. The parser hands each consumer
its own typed keys, the typed-key surface is the boundary, and any
helper that crosses it is the same regression as an in-code backend
registry coming back through a different door.

**`aide_brain`'s `kind` vocabulary is closed at exactly the two live
sections.** The tool accepts `kind?: "orientation" | "config"` and
no other value. `kind: "orientation"` is the default and returns the
runtime briefing; `kind: "config"` returns the wiring flow
`/aide:brain config` executes. Adding `kind: "playbook-index"`,
`kind: "study-playbook"`, `kind: "update-playbook"`, `kind:
"research-index"`, or any future seed section would expose a seed
through the live-section interface — the failure mode the live-vs-seed
split exists to prevent. The schema's split is enforceable at the
tool surface or it is not enforced at all; the closed `kind` enum is
the enforcement.

**update-playbook is a seed because consistency with study-playbook
beats convenience.** Every methodology that runs against the brain —
playbook-navigation, playbook-maintenance — is the user's content,
edited in the user's brain, evolving outside the package's release
cycle. The shipped `/aide:update-playbook` command and the agent-side
`study-playbook` skill share one shape: a thin pointer at an on-disk
brain artifact whose body holds the methodology. Making
`update-playbook` a seed (lands in the brain at
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` on cold install, edited in the
brain afterwards) preserves that shape. Making it a live `aide_brain`
kind would split the playbook-related methodology surfaces in two —
one read live from `brain.aide`, one read on-disk from the brain —
and force users to learn a different edit surface for each. The
consistency-with-study-playbook decision is load-bearing precisely
because the user's mental model for "where do I edit this" must
extend cleanly across both methodologies.

**The cold-install CLI is argument-less and brain-agnostic.** `npx
aidemd-mcp init` accepts no brain-shaped flag of any kind. It
installs the methodology layer only — methodology docs, pipeline
agent files, slash commands, skills, the framework pointer stub, and
the IDE preview wiring — and touches no brain surface. It does not
write `.aide/config/brain.aide`. It does not write a `.mcp.json`
entry under `mcpServers["brain"]`. It does not call the brain
provisioner. It does not create the brain root directory. It does
not ask the user for a brain path, an API token, or any other
backend-specific value. Brain wiring is a separate user-driven
action on the first `/aide` run, and the cold install's terminal
warning routes the user there explicitly. Splitting wiring out of
install is what lets the install path stay backend-agnostic across
the catalog of brains the user could possibly wire — an install path
that knows about path slots is an install path that has to know
about token slots, page-id slots, and connection-uri slots the
moment the catalog grows. Future integration-selection at install
time (an `npx aidemd-mcp init --brain <integration>` form that
selects which bundled template scaffolds on first config) is a
forward-compatible extension that still leaves slot-filling to
`/aide:brain config` — the install path never collects
backend-specific values.

**Brain wiring is `/aide:brain config`'s job, and `aide_init({
category: "brain" })` is unconditional inside that router.** The
shipped `/aide:brain config` calls `aide_init({ category: "brain"
})` at the top of every invocation. The call is not gated on the
brain's status, not branched on whether `brain.aide` already exists,
not skipped when entry-point seed artifacts already exist on disk.
The unconditional call is what makes missing-file recovery
self-heal: the brain provisioner's presence-only idempotency
re-seeds any missing artifact in place without touching anything
that already exists. After the unconditional call the router pulls
integration-specific wiring instructions via `aide_brain({ kind:
"config" })` and executes them verbatim, forwarding any user
`$ARGUMENTS` opaquely. Folding recovery into `config`'s preamble
keeps the brain command surface minimal at one verb — there is no
`doctor`, no `repair`, no `status` subcommand the agent has to know
to invoke. A conditional call would push recovery into a separate
verb the user and agent both have to discover; the unconditional
call hides recovery behind the verb they already know to run.

**YAML null is the unwired-slot signal, not a string.** The bundled
template's `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]` —
every element is either a fully-formed argument string or YAML null
(a bare `-` with no scalar after, which YAML parses to `null`). A
null at any index means the user has not yet supplied the value
that slot needs. The unwired state is a structural property of the
config, not a string the package has to recognize. A literal-string
sentinel under any name is rejected: a sentinel produces a
configuration where `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` agree byte-for-byte
(both contain the literal sentinel), the drift detector returns
`ok`, the orchestrator never routes to `/aide:brain config`, and
the user discovers the broken state only when the brain MCP server
fails to launch. YAML null cannot pass the drift check and cannot
land in `.mcp.json` as a launchable arg — the structural signal
forces every consumer to either fill the slot or refuse, with no
silent middle path. Substitution surfaces stay separate: `${...}`
interpolation of frontmatter fields into args remains for advanced
users, but the unwired-slot signal does not pass through any
substitution surface — null is null until the user edits it.

**Per-integration ownership of arg-index slot semantics.** The
meaning of each slot in `mcpServerConfig.args` — which index is a
path, which is a token, which is a connection URI, which is already
fully wired by the launcher itself — is documented exclusively
inside that integration's `config` body section in its bundled
`brain.aide` template. The bundled Obsidian default names `args[3]`
as the absolute path to the user's brain root; a future Mem0
integration would name `args[2]` as the API token and `args[3]` as
the user identifier in its own template. Whatever future
integrations exist, each describes its own slot map in its own
prose. The shipped `/aide:brain config` router NEVER introspects
which slot is which — it forwards `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely and lets
the integration's prose drive every per-slot decision. There is no
shipped registry of slot meanings, no per-index validation table,
no `argSlots` frontmatter field declaring what each index is
supposed to be. Slot semantics live in the user-owned body, same
as every other per-backend concern. This is what makes adding a
new backend a host-side prose addition rather than a package code
change.

**Sync is byte-faithful for real args and refusing for null args.**
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` reads `brain.aide` and writes the
post-interpolation `mcpServerConfig` byte-for-byte into
`.mcp.json`'s `mcpServers["brain"]` entry. Sync is idempotent. Sync
refuses to write null-bearing args: if any element of
`mcpServerConfig.args` is null at sync time, sync exits non-zero,
names the offending index(es), and routes the user to `/aide:brain
config` to fill them. Propagating null into a launch command is
propagating broken state, not a useful byte-faithful action. A sync
that silently coerces null to an empty string, drops the null
entry, substitutes a placeholder, or otherwise converts the
unwired-slot signal into a string is exactly the regression that
made literal-sentinel designs unworkable in the first place. The
MCP server never silently rewrites `.mcp.json`; mutation of the
host's MCP wiring is always a deliberate user action with a visible
command boundary.

**Boot reporter brain status is a closed four-state vocabulary; null
args collapse into `no-mcp-entry`.** The brain status states are
`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift`. The
`buildBrainState` helper maps any null entry in
`mcpServerConfig.args` to `no-mcp-entry` semantically: a
null-bearing config is by definition not a wireable launch command,
so the brain's MCP entry is treated as not-yet-wired regardless of
whether `.mcp.json` happens to contain a stale entry from a prior
session. The four-state vocabulary is closed; introducing a fifth
state for null-armed args (`unwired`, `incomplete-args`,
`placeholder-present`) would force every consumer of the four-state
union — orchestrator routing, the `/aide` flow, the boot reporter —
to grow a fifth branch with no different remedy than `no-mcp-entry`
already has (route to `/aide:brain config`). Collapsing the
unwired-slot case into `no-mcp-entry` is the design that keeps the
state space minimal and the orchestrator routing trivial.

**Shipped pipeline commands forward arguments, they do not interpret
them.** `/aide:brain config` takes `$ARGUMENTS` as an opaque
pass-through string and forwards it verbatim to the integration's
`config` prose. The shipped command does not parse the bytes, does
not validate the bytes, does not document what shape the bytes
should take. Whether the bytes mean an absolute path, an API token,
a `token+pageId` JSON blob, or anything else is the integration's
concern, documented inside the integration's `config` body section
in `brain.aide`. The shipped command's docs cannot list parameter
shapes — there are none at the shipped layer. A shipped command
that hardcodes a positional argument because one bundled
integration happens to want one is the in-code backend registry
coming back through the command-prompt surface; the same regression
in different clothing as a switch on `connector` or a registry
lookup keyed by name. `/aide:update-playbook` applies the same rule
at one step deeper: it calls `aide_brain` for the brain's read
tool, reads `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and follows that
file's instructions verbatim. Backend-specific MCP tool names, edit
recipes, and routing-table mechanics live in the on-disk artifact,
not in the shipped command file.

**Pre-rework files return `malformed-body`; there is no transitional
read path and no auto-rewrite.** Any `brain.aide` file that does
not present all six new marker pairs in required order halts the
pipeline with `malformed-body` naming the missing markers
specifically. There is no `aide_upgrade` carve-out that auto-injects
missing pairs, no transitional reader that renames retired markers
on the fly, no fallback path that synthesizes missing sections.
`brain.aide` is user-owned the moment it lands, and any code path
that mutates it on a subsequent run shifts a load-bearing user
surface out from under the user — the same regression class as
`aide_init` rewriting `.aide/config/` files or the MCP server
silently rewriting `.mcp.json`. Strict failure that names every
missing marker, plus the user hand-editing, is the only acceptable
migration path.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across every code surface this package
authors, with four explicit carve-outs and no fifth.** The forbidden
tokens — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (as a synonym for "file"),
`wikilink`, `hub note`, `hub` — must not appear in any user-facing
prose surface this package authors (MCP tool descriptions, the
README, canonical methodology docs, slash-command prompts, agent
definitions, every `.aide` spec authored in this repo) nor in code
identifiers, comments, or string constants project-wide. The four
allowed exceptions each name a legitimate non-branching anchor: the
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name (describes bundled
template content, not a dispatch axis), the `.obsidian` directory
heuristic comment in `brainRootExists` (an honestly-labeled
implementation detail in a single helper), the legacy `obsidian`
MCP key referenced inside migration branches (the key being
migrated away from), and the body section bytes of the bundled
`obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper itself across all six named
sections. The fourth carve-out exists because the bundled template
IS the obsidian default: those bytes ship obsidian-flavored
content by design across every section, they become user-owned the
moment they are scaffolded onto disk or seeded into the brain, and
any host that wires a different brain edits the section bytes in
their own `brain.aide`. The bundled-template-body-bytes carve-out
is scoped exactly: it covers the bytes inside the template's body
section literals only — NOT the JSDoc, identifiers, parameter
names, or function names in the template module's `index.ts`, NOT
string constants anywhere outside the body section literals, NOT
anti-example bullets in undesired-outcome blocks (those have their
own anti-example carve-out), and NOT any other bundled template
should one ever exist unless it is explicitly named as another
connector-flavored default. Anti-example occurrences inside
undesired-outcome blocks are also allowed — naming a forbidden
token to forbid it is the mechanism, not a violation. The safe-list
of storage-agnostic body-field names is exactly `orientation`,
`config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex`; the `Hub`-suffix regression class extends across
every seed-section field. Every other appearance of the forbidden
tokens, in code or prose, is a regression.

**Migration from the brain is one-way.** Every doc that lands in
this repo is deleted or archived from the brain on the same commit.
Drift between two copies of a canonical doc is the worst kind of
drift, because it silently propagates to every host project on its
next init and no one notices which copy is authoritative.

**Intent-driven development is the measurement.** Every change to this
repo — a new doc, a new tool, a revised description, a refactored helper
— is evaluated against one question: does this make "describe what you
want in cascading layers of abstraction, let the agent build the code"
more real or less real? A feature that adds surface but doesn't move that
needle is a regression in disguise. A change that tightens the cascading
intent model is load-bearing even when it looks like cleanup.

## Good examples

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify how outcomes cascade
from parent to child specs. They commit. On the next release, every
host project that runs `aide_init` sees the updated text land inside
its own `.aide/aide-spec.md` — the host-side doc hub contains a
byte-faithful copy of the canonical doc, so the edit reaches every
host project on its next init with no duplication anywhere in between.
Every agent that connects to the MCP server also sees the updated tool
descriptions (in whichever tools the rule touched), because those
descriptions were composed from the same doc file at build time. One
edit in one file, full propagation, zero paraphrasing, and the stub
inside each host's config file keeps pointing at the same hub path it
always did.

An agent in a cold host project is told only "run aide_init." It does,
reads back a summary naming the pointer stub in the config file, the
installed host-side doc hub with every canonical methodology doc by
name, the pipeline slash commands, the MCP registration, and the
IDE configuration, plus a terminal warning that brain wiring still
needs to happen via `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run.
At no point in that sequence did a human tell the agent what AIDE
is, what a `.aide` file is, or how the phases fit together — every
piece came through the tooling. The summary is honest about what the
install did and did not do: the methodology layer is in place,
`brain.aide` does not yet exist, the `.mcp.json` brain entry has not
been written, and the next step is the user-facing one the warning
named.

An agent opens a host project to implement a new feature unrelated to
AIDE. Its first read is the host's config file, which contains a short
pointer stub saying AIDE exists, naming the doc-hub path, and telling
the agent to crawl the hub before writing or acting on any `.aide`
file. The agent notes this, skips the hub for now because the task
touches no specs, and proceeds with the feature work carrying only a
handful of lines of AIDE context. An hour later, the same agent picks
up a task that does touch a `.aide` file; it follows the stub's
pointer into the hub, reads the hub index, drills into `aide-spec.md`
and `aide-template.md`, and leaves the rest of the canonical docs its
task does not need unread. The delivery layer practiced the same
progressive disclosure the methodology teaches — exactly the way the
user's global `study-playbook` skill crawls the brain on demand
instead of hoisting it into every session.

A contributor adds a new tool to the MCP server. They read `.aide/intent.aide`,
narrow to `src/.aide`, narrow to their new submodule spec, and know
exactly which invariants their addition must honor: one-to-one mapping
between methodology phases and tools, client parity, idempotency,
progressive disclosure in the code, cascading intent in the spec.
A small number of reads, full contract, no source files opened.

The user runs `npx aidemd-mcp init` in a brand-new host project. The
CLI installs the methodology layer end-to-end: writes the pointer
stub into the framework config file, lands the canonical methodology
doc set under `.aide/docs/`, scaffolds the pipeline slash commands
under `.claude/commands/aide/`, installs the pipeline agent files
under `.claude/agents/aide/`, installs the generic `study-playbook`
skill, writes the `aide` MCP server entry into `.mcp.json`, configures
IDE preview wiring. It does not write `.aide/config/brain.aide`. It
does not write a `.mcp.json` entry under `mcpServers["brain"]`. It
does not create a brain root directory. The terminal output ends
with a warning that names `/aide:brain config` as the surface to
run on first `/aide` to wire the brain, plus any IDE-choice or
upgrade follow-ups the install deferred. The user closes the
terminal knowing exactly what was done and what is left.

The user runs `/aide` for the first time in the same project. The
orchestrator reads boot state, sees brain status `no-brain-aide`,
and routes to `/aide:brain config`. The shipped `/aide:brain config`
router calls `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` unconditionally as
its first step. The brain provisioner runs its presence-only
idempotency check across its seven plan steps, and because nothing
brain-shaped exists yet, it scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide`
with the bundled obsidian default, ensures the brain root
directories exist, seeds the four entry-point artifacts from the
template's seed sections, and writes the `.mcp.json` brain entry
derived from the scaffolded `brain.aide` via the parser. The
scaffolded `brain.aide` lands with two top-level frontmatter fields
and a body laid out as six marker-bounded sections in the required
order — `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` — each opened and closed by its
own pair of HTML-comment markers. The frontmatter has YAML null at
the path slot of `mcpServerConfig.args`, since the user has not yet
supplied a brain root path:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: cmd
  args:
    - /c
    - npx
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    -
---
```

The bare `-` on the last line is YAML null, parsed as the explicit
unwired-slot signal. `name: obsidian` is a label the user reads in
the file and the agent narrates back in conversation; the package
never branches on it. The body section bytes ship obsidian-flavored
content because the bundled template IS the obsidian default,
under the storage-agnostic-vocabulary carve-out for the template's
body section literals:

```
<!-- aide-orientation-start -->
Your brain is an Obsidian-backed knowledge store reached through the
MCP labeled `brain`. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by
their brain-relative path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for
keyword queries. The store has four entry-point artifacts: the
coding-playbook index at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the
study-playbook navigation guide at
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, the update-playbook
maintenance guide at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and the
research index at `research/research.md`. Start from the relevant
entry-point for your task and follow the `[[wikilinks]]` it lists
to deepen context.
<!-- aide-orientation-end -->

<!-- aide-config-start -->
You are completing the wiring of an Obsidian brain. The required
value is the absolute path to the user's Obsidian vault. In
`mcpServerConfig.args` for this integration, `args[3]` is the
path slot — the index that holds the brain-root path the launcher
needs.

Argument shape (Obsidian only): `/aide:brain config [<absolute-path>]`.

1. Read `brain.aide`. Inspect `mcpServerConfig.args[3]`.
   - If `args[3]` is null, the brain is not wired; resolve a target
     path from `$ARGUMENTS` (non-empty) or by asking the user
     (empty).
   - If `args[3]` is a real path and `$ARGUMENTS` is empty, STOP —
     there is nothing to do.
   - If `args[3]` is a real path and `$ARGUMENTS` is non-empty, the
     user is re-pointing the brain; the supplied value becomes the
     new target.
2. Edit `brain.aide` — replace `args[3]` with the resolved target
   path string.
3. Sync — read `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]`, append `"sync"` to
   its args, run via Bash.
4. Emit the restart message.
<!-- aide-config-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->
# Coding Playbook

## Task Routing

| Task domain | Section |
|-------------|---------|

## Sections
<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->

<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->
# Study Playbook

**Step 1.** Open `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`. The Task
Routing table tells you which section applies.

**Step 2.** Drill into the matching section. Read its hub note and
follow the `[[wikilinks]]` it lists.

**Step 3.** Follow picked wikilinks into target notes. Read only
what your task demands.
<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->
# Update Playbook

Maintenance methodology. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` and
`mcp__brain__patch_note` against entries under `coding-playbook/`.

1. Identify the change.
2. Read `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` to locate the section.
3. Apply the change.
4. Routing-table drift check: compare the index's routing table
   against the actual sections.
5. Reconcile any drift the user approves.
<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-index-start -->
# Research

## Domains

| Domain | Notes |
|--------|-------|
<!-- aide-research-index-end -->
```

The marker layout is what the parser enforces: lowercase,
case-sensitive, distinct open and close per section, in the fixed
six-pair order. The body text inside each pair is connector-flavored
by design here because the bundled template IS the obsidian default —
the carve-out for the template's body section literals legitimately
permits the obsidian vocabulary across all six sections. `aide_brain`
reads the `orientation` and `config` sections live; the brain
provisioner reads the `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` sections once at install time
to scaffold the four entry-point artifacts inside the brain. After
install, the brain owns those four files; the seed sections in
`brain.aide` are dormant. The MCP entry the brain provisioner
writes to `.mcp.json` is byte-faithful to `mcpServerConfig` from
the scaffolded `brain.aide` — null at `args[3]` carries through to
`.mcp.json` unchanged at this point, because filling the slot is
the next user-facing step in the same flow, not a sync operation.

The same `/aide:brain config` invocation continues past its
unconditional `aide_init` call: it pulls the integration's wiring
instructions via `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`, executes them
verbatim with `$ARGUMENTS` forwarded. The integration's prose
inspects `args[3]`, sees null, recognizes the user's
`$ARGUMENTS` as the target absolute path (or asks for one if
`$ARGUMENTS` is empty), edits `brain.aide` to replace null with
the supplied path string, and runs sync via the `mcpServers.aide`
invocation it pulled from `.mcp.json`. Sync reads the now-fully-
populated `mcpServerConfig`, sees no null entries, writes the
launch command into `.mcp.json` byte-for-byte, and exits clean.
The shipped `/aide:brain config` file never parsed the path
argument, never named a backend, never inspected which arg index
was the path slot — the integration's `config` prose did all of
it.

An agent connects to the host's MCP server at session start and
calls `aide_brain()` with no arguments. The tool defaults `kind` to
`"orientation"`, parses `brain.aide`, extracts the bytes between
`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` and `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->`,
and returns them verbatim with `status: "ok"`. The agent reads the
prose, learns that the brain is an Obsidian-backed store with four
entry-point artifacts at the listed paths, and from there can call
`mcp__brain__read_note` against any of them. At no point did the
server know what kind of backend was wired; the bytes the agent read
came verbatim from the user's own `brain.aide`.

A user runs `/aide:update-playbook` to refine the playbook's testing
section. The shipped command is a one-step router: it calls
`aide_brain` once, learns from the `orientation` section that the
brain's read tool is `mcp__brain__read_note`, reads
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` from the brain, and follows
the five-step methodology that file describes (identify the change,
read the index, apply the change, drift check, reconcile). The
shipped command file never named `mcp__brain__patch_note`, never
described routing-table drift detection, never branched on the
wired backend — all of that lives in the on-disk update-playbook
artifact, which the user has already edited twice since install to
refine for their team's conventions.

A second host wires a different brain — a hosted knowledge base
reached through a different MCP — and edits their own `brain.aide`
end-to-end. The frontmatter `name` becomes a label appropriate to
the new backend; `mcpServerConfig.command` and `mcpServerConfig.args`
become whatever launcher the new backend wants, with YAML null at
whichever args indexes the user has not yet filled. The
`orientation` section now names the hosted backend's MCP tools; the
`config` section documents the new backend's slot map (e.g.
"args[2] is the API token, args[3] is the user identifier") and
the wiring flow's edit-and-sync sequence; the `studyPlaybook` and
`updatePlaybook` seeds describe page-link traversal and API-driven
page edits respectively; the `researchIndex` Notes column becomes
Pages. The package's code surface — every identifier, JSDoc word,
log string, InitStep `name` field — stays storage-agnostic
regardless. Neither shipped command file changed; both still
forward to whatever the host's brain says, and `/aide:brain config`
still calls `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` unconditionally and
forwards `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely.

The user makes a one-line edit to their fully-wired `brain.aide` —
they retarget the brain by changing the path string inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args` and rerun `npx aidemd-mcp sync`. Sync reads
the file's frontmatter, takes `mcpServerConfig` byte-for-byte as
the user wrote it, sees no null entries, and writes the resulting
object into `.mcp.json` under the `brain` key. A second `sync`
immediately afterward computes the same target, sees `.mcp.json`
already matches, and exits reporting no change. The host's MCP
wiring was mutated exactly once, on a command the user typed.

## Bad examples

A doctrine change that lands as a one-liner in a host project's CLAUDE.md
after a maintainer "noticed something while using AIDE in production."
The host project is correct for a week. The canonical docs in this repo
still reflect the old rule. The next host project that runs `aide_init`
receives the old rule because `aide_init` reads from the canonical docs,
and the two projects now diverge invisibly. The fix is to land the
change in this repo first and let it flow outward, not to patch the edge
and forget to come back.

A `writeMethodology/` helper that builds its output by concatenating
hardcoded string literals instead of reading `docs/aide-spec.md`. The
canonical doc and the shipped methodology drift apart on the first edit
to either one, and a maintainer updating the doc has no way to know
their change did not ship. The repo has two sources of truth and
neither is marked authoritative.

An `aide_init` that skips the doc-hub step and dumps the full
concatenated methodology body inside the host's config file, the way
it used to before this architecture was locked in. The agent pays the
full doctrine cost on every read of the config file, the doctrine
crowds out the host's own content, and the methodology's own
progressive-disclosure rule is violated by the very tool whose job
is to install it. The refactor passed its tests and broke the
invariant it was supposed to enforce.

A pointer stub that names a host-side doc-hub path at `.aide/` while
the installer writes the hub into `aide/` (or any other mismatch of
the same shape). The agent reads the stub, follows the pointer, and
lands on a missing directory. The two sides were coincident string
literals instead of a single shared path source, and the refactor
shipped with its most load-bearing invariant broken at compile time.

A proposal for `aide_explain`, a new tool that "walks an agent through
the methodology interactively." The teaching surface now gains a third
delivery channel — handshake, install action, and now explain — and an
agent calling explain is an agent the tool descriptions failed to teach.
The correct response is to fix the tool descriptions, not to add a
channel that papers over their weakness.

A canonical doc that introduces a new pipeline phase — say, "review" —
without `aide_init` scaffolding an `/aide:review` slash command to
match. Agents read the methodology, look for the command, can't find
it, and conclude the system is incomplete. The doc was aspirational;
the tooling was real; they disagreed; the agent trusted the tooling
and lost trust in the doc.

A child `.aide` that opens with "this repo is the canonical home of
AIDE…" — a verbatim restatement of this spec's intent paragraph. The
child has earned nothing by copying; every token spent restating is a
token the child could have used to say what only it can say. Cascading
intent failed because the child didn't cascade.

`aide_brain`'s implementation peeks at `mcpServers["brain"]` in the
host's `.mcp.json`, inspects the entry's `command` and `args` shape,
matches the args against a registered table of "known launchers,"
identifies a specific connector, and returns connector-specific prose
hardcoded in the package's source. The package now has to know about
every brain a user might ever wire — a registry coming in through the
side door. A user who wires an MCP server whose launcher shape doesn't
match any registered matcher gets a "no brain wired" status from the
boot reporter even though they clearly wired one. Re-introducing the
matching under any name — registry, matcher table, identity inferrer,
"connector detection helper" — is the same regression in different
clothing.

The boot reporter runs at server startup, detects that `brain.aide`
and `.mcp.json` disagree, and "helpfully" auto-syncs by overwriting
`.mcp.json`'s brain entry from the `brain.aide` source of truth. The
user wakes up to a `.mcp.json` mutated by something that was not a
command they typed; their prior-afternoon hand-edit pointing the
brain MCP server at a temporary debug location is silently gone.
Drift is a hard halt, not a self-repair event; the only thing the
boot reporter is allowed to do with drift is name it as `mcp-drift`
and stop.

A shipped `.claude/commands/aide/brain.md` file documents
`<absolute-path>` as a positional argument under its config mode.
The shipped command's prose says "pass the absolute path to your
brain root as `$ARGUMENTS`," and parses `$ARGUMENTS` against a
path-shape expectation before forwarding. The bundled obsidian
default happens to want a path — but the shipped command is the
wrong layer to know that. A different brain wants a token-and-id
pair, a hosted-API brain wants an API key, a JSON-blob brain wants
a JSON blob; documenting one backend's shape at the shipped layer
makes every other backend's users wrong by default and forces them
to ignore docs that contradict their own integration's `config`
section. The shipped command must take `$ARGUMENTS` opaquely,
forward verbatim, and let the integration's `config` prose document
its own argument shape and slot map. A command that parses
`$ARGUMENTS` against a backend-specific shape is the in-code
backend registry coming back through the command surface.

A maintainer adds a third value to `aide_brain`'s `kind` enum:
`kind: "update-playbook"` returns the `updatePlaybook` body section
verbatim. The motivation reads as "consistency — if `orientation`
and `config` are reachable, why not `update-playbook` too?" The
failure mode is exactly the dual-source-of-truth problem the
live-vs-seed split exists to prevent: after install, the
authoritative bytes for the update-playbook methodology live in the
brain at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, where the user has
edited them twice. `aide_brain({ kind: "update-playbook" })`
returns the `brain.aide` seed bytes, which are now stale relative
to the brain file. The agent reads the live tool's response and
follows methodology the user replaced months ago. The live-vs-seed
split must be enforceable at the tool surface or it is not
enforceable at all; the closed two-value `kind` enum is the
enforcement. Extending `kind` to `playbook-index`,
`study-playbook`, or `research-index` is the same regression for
the same reason.

A maintainer notices that some `brain.aide` files in the wild use
an older marker grammar and adds a transitional read path: when the
parser sees old pairs, it auto-renames them to the new pairs in
memory, synthesizes empty new sections, and returns `ok` so the
pipeline proceeds. To avoid a per-session penalty the maintainer
takes one more step: `aide_upgrade` rewrites `brain.aide` on disk,
replacing old markers with new ones and inserting empty blocks. The
user's file is now mutated by code they did not run, with empty
sections that look authored but aren't — the next time
`/aide:brain config` calls `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` it
gets back zero bytes and silently does nothing. `brain.aide` is
user-owned the moment it lands; mutating it from any code path on
a subsequent run shifts a load-bearing user surface out from under
the user — same regression class as the MCP server silently
rewriting `.mcp.json`. Strict `malformed-body` failure that names
every missing marker and forces the user to hand-edit is the only
acceptable migration path.

The install service helper reads `brain.aide` and grabs the
`orientation` field off the parsed result to decide what to seed
the playbook index with. The motivation reads as "compose the
playbook index from the orientation prose so the user only has to
edit one place." The boundary the architecture draws is the
opposite: the install service reads only seed sections
(`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex`) and never the live ones. A symmetric regression:
`aide_brain` reads the `studyPlaybook` field off the parsed result
to compose its `kind: "orientation"` response, on the theory that
"every entry-point fact the orientation needs to mention is already
in the study-playbook seed, so let's not duplicate." Both
directions cross the per-consumer ownership boundary, both create
a path where one consumer's edits silently affect the other
consumer's output, and both rebuild the very dual-source-of-truth
problem the split exists to prevent. The parser hands each
consumer its own typed keys; helpers on either side touch only
their own keys.

A maintainer reads the storage-agnostic-vocabulary carve-out for
the bundled template's body section literals and concludes the
carve-out is a license to use connector-flavored vocabulary
anywhere in the template module. JSDoc on the template's exported
function picks up "returns the obsidian-flavored brain.aide bytes
for vault scaffolding"; an internal helper becomes
`buildHubBlock`; a string constant outside the body section
literals declares `const HUB_HEADER = "## Hub"`; a log line in
the brain provisioner reads `provisioning vault root at...`. The
carve-out covers BYTES inside the body section literals only —
NOT JSDoc, NOT identifiers, NOT parameter names, NOT function
names, NOT string constants outside the section literals. The
package's code surface stays storage-agnostic regardless of what
the user-owned section bytes ship; conflating "the bundled
template's body bytes ship obsidian content" with "the template
module's code surface gets to as well" is the same regression as
letting connector vocabulary leak into the brain provisioner's
identifiers.

A maintainer extends the parser's result type with new fields for
the rework's sections and writes the type as `{ frontmatter,
orientation, config, playbookIndexHub, studyPlaybookHub,
updatePlaybookHub, researchIndexHub }`. The `Hub` suffix is a
retired regression class — adding it to any seed-section field
reintroduces the rejected shape under new section names. The
kebab-case marker tokens populate the camelCase field names
exactly: `playbook-index → playbookIndex`, `update-playbook →
updatePlaybook`, `research-index → researchIndex`. The safe-list
of storage-agnostic body-field names is exactly `orientation`,
`config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex`; the `Hub` suffix is wrong wherever it lands.

`cli/init` accepts a brain-shaped path flag that, when set, writes
`.aide/config/brain.aide` with the supplied path inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args` and writes a matching `.mcp.json` brain
entry as part of the cold install. The motivation reads as
"convenience for the power user who already knows their brain
root." The failure mode is structural: collecting a backend-
specific value at install time forces the install path to know
about path slots, and a path slot is a path slot is a path slot
until the catalog of brains the user could possibly wire grows
to include token slots and connection-uri slots. The argument
shape that worked for one bundled default becomes the argument
shape every future bundled integration has to fight against. The
brain-agnostic install path is structural: it ships only the
methodology layer, and brain wiring lives downstream of the
first `/aide` run where `/aide:brain config` owns it. Forms of
the same regression include any path-shaped flag, any token-
shaped flag for a hosted backend, an MCP-config-blob flag, and
any other backend-shaped flag on the install CLI under any name.

The bundled obsidian template emits `mcpServerConfig.args` with a
recognizable literal string at the path slot when the brain has
not been wired — angle-bracketed bytes the wiring flow is meant
to grep-replace on its way to landing the real path. The intent
reads as "use a visible placeholder so the user knows where to
substitute." The failure mode is exactly what makes literal-
sentinel designs unworkable: `brain.aide` lands with the literal
in args, sync runs, the byte-identical literal propagates into
`.mcp.json` under `mcpServers["brain"]`, the boot reporter
compares `brain.aide` against `.mcp.json`, they agree byte-for-
byte, the brain status returns `ok`, and the orchestrator never
routes to `/aide:brain config`. The user discovers the broken
state only when the brain MCP server fails to launch on a string
the launcher cannot interpret. YAML null is the structural
unwired-slot signal precisely because it cannot pass the drift
check and cannot land in `.mcp.json` as a launchable arg — every
consumer either fills the slot or refuses, with no silent middle
path. Re-introducing a literal sentinel under any name — angle-
bracketed placeholder, double-underscore-flanked uppercase token,
magic UUID, the integer `-1`, the empty string treated as
unwired, or any other in-band string the package recognizes as
"please fill me in" — is the same regression class.

`cli/sync` reads `brain.aide`, sees a null entry in
`mcpServerConfig.args`, and "helpfully" coerces it to the empty
string before writing `.mcp.json`. The motivation reads as
"byte-faithful propagation — `args` is `(string | null)[]`, so
the closest string is the empty string." The failure mode is
that `.mcp.json` now contains a launch command with an empty arg
where the brain root path was supposed to go, the launcher
either crashes on the empty value or interprets it as the
current working directory, and the boot reporter reports `ok`
because `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` agree (the `null →
""` round-trip is stable). Variants of the same regression: sync
drops the null entry entirely (shifting every later index by one
and breaking slot semantics for every integration), substitutes a
placeholder string, or rewrites `brain.aide` to fill the null
with a default. The contract is hard-fail: any null arg at sync
time exits non-zero, names the offending index, and routes the
user to `/aide:brain config`. Sync's job is byte-faithful
propagation of fully-formed launch commands, not best-effort
patching of broken state.

A `/aide:brain config` router that calls `aide_init({ category:
"brain" })` only when `aide_info.brain.status` is
`no-brain-aide`, skipping the call when `brain.aide` already
exists or when the four entry-point artifacts are already on
disk. The motivation reads as "avoid redundant work — the brain
provisioner has nothing to do if the artifacts exist." The
failure mode is that a user who deleted a seed artifact, whose
prior install was interrupted, or whose template version added a
section gets no recovery: the conditional skips the
provisioner's presence-only idempotency check, the missing
artifact stays missing, and the user has to discover a separate
recovery verb. The unconditional call is what makes recovery
self-heal. A conditional call pushes recovery into a `doctor` /
`repair` / `status` subcommand the agent has to know to invoke,
splitting the brain command surface and forcing every documentation
surface to teach the conditional. The brain command surface stays
minimal at one verb (`config`); recovery folds into that verb's
preamble.

A boot reporter or `buildBrainState` mapping that introduces a
fifth status state for null-armed args — `unwired`,
`incomplete-args`, `placeholder-present`, or any other label that
splits the unwired-slot case off from `no-mcp-entry`. The
motivation reads as "give the user a more specific signal." The
failure mode is that the four-state vocabulary stops being closed:
every consumer of the union (orchestrator routing, the `/aide`
flow, the boot reporter, every test that exhaustively branches on
the union) grows a fifth branch that routes to the same remedy
`no-mcp-entry` already routes to (`/aide:brain config`).
Specificity that doesn't change the remedy is overhead, not
information. Null-armed args collapse semantically into
`no-mcp-entry` because a null arg means the brain's MCP entry is
not yet wireable; the four-state vocabulary stays closed.

A shipped command file, code path, or registry that knows which
arg index in `mcpServerConfig.args` means what — a switch on
`args[3]` because the bundled default's path lives there, a
matcher recognizing token-shaped strings at `args[2]`, an
`argSlots` frontmatter field declaring "this index is a path,"
a `/aide:brain config` router that introspects which slots are
null to decide what to ask the user for. The motivation reads as
"validate the slot before sync runs." The failure mode is the
same as every previous registry regression: the package now has
an opinion about which slots in which integrations mean what,
and that opinion has to be updated every time a new integration's
slot map changes. Slot semantics live exclusively in the
per-integration `config` body section — the package never
inspects which slot is which. A command or code path that grows
arg-index awareness is the in-code backend registry coming back
through the slot surface.

## References

- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.aide/intent.aide` (frontmatter, lines 1-704) -- the source-of-truth contract this body synthesizes from. Every Strategy decision traces to a frontmatter outcome: the YAML-null-as-unwired-slot-signal paragraph traces to the args-typed `(string | null)[]` outcome and the no-literal-sentinel undesired outcome; the cli/init brain-agnostic paragraph traces to the cold-install-CLI-is-argument-less outcome and the cli/init-writing-brain-surface undesired outcome; the unconditional-`aide_init` paragraph traces to the shipped-pipeline-commands outcome; the per-integration arg-index slot semantics paragraph traces to the corresponding outcome of the same name; the sync-refusal paragraph traces to the `brain.aide`-and-`.mcp.json`-stay-in-sync outcome and the cli/sync-null-bearing-args undesired outcome; the closed-four-state paragraph traces to the no-`rootPath`-no-fifth-state outcome and the boot-reporter-fifth-state undesired outcome.
- `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` -- canonical AIDE spec format conventions: required body sections, frontmatter rules, the rule that strategy decisions trace to outcomes, and the decisions-not-descriptions standard for `## Strategy` paragraphs. Informed the per-paragraph rewrite shape: each strategy paragraph leads with the decision and follows with the reasoning.
- `.aide/docs/index.md` -- canonical doc set inventory; informed the implicit commitment that `brain-aide.md` tracks the six-section / four-artifact contract and that `brain.aide` is a documented AIDE file type alongside `intent.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide`, `research.aide`.
- `src/cli/init/.aide` -- sibling spec for the cold-install CLI whose own description was rewritten to "argument-less, brain-agnostic" in the same Stage 1 frontmatter rewrite. Confirmed the framing for the brain-agnostic-install Strategy paragraph: methodology-only install, no brain surfaces, terminal warning routes to `/aide:brain config`.
- `src/cli/sync/.aide` -- sibling spec for the sync verb whose description was rewritten to "refuses to write null-bearing args" in the same Stage 1 rewrite. Confirmed the sync-refusal Strategy paragraph's "exit non-zero, name the offending index, route to `/aide:brain config`" remedy is the contract the sync module enforces.
- `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` -- sibling spec for the brain-state detector whose description was rewritten to "returns `no-mcp-entry` immediately when any element of the parsed `mcpServerConfig.args` is null" in the same Stage 1 rewrite. Confirmed the closed-four-state Strategy paragraph's null-collapses-to-`no-mcp-entry` mapping is the contract `buildBrainState` enforces and that the null check runs before drift comparison.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- sibling spec for the parser whose description names `mcpServerConfig.args` typed `(string | null)[]` with null preserved verbatim through to consumers. Confirmed the YAML-null Strategy paragraph's parser-level contract: null is part of the args type, the parser does not coerce or reject, downstream consumers decide per their own contracts.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` -- sibling spec for the brain provisioner whose description names presence-only idempotency, the seven-step plan, and brain-root-path derivation via `parseBrainAide`. Confirmed the unconditional-`aide_init` Strategy paragraph's recovery-self-heals mechanism: the provisioner's idempotency is what makes the unconditional call free when nothing is missing.
- `src/tools/brain/.aide` -- sibling spec for the `aide_brain` MCP tool whose description names the closed two-value `kind` discriminator and the response shape that carries only `status` + `instructions`. Confirmed the closed-`kind` Strategy paragraph's enforcement: the schema's split is enforceable at the tool surface or it is not enforced at all.
- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- architectural decision sign-off captured during the rework. Carries the anti-regression invariants every build phase must hold (no transitional read paths, no silent acceptance of marker-layout violations, no introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args`, no reading another consumer's owned body section, no shipped pipeline command file documenting integration-specific argument shape, no hardcoded sync invocation in any skill or command prompt). Informed the bad-examples set's anti-pattern catalog.

(External domain research was not drawn for this rework. The design conclusions were specified directly in the frontmatter rewrite — the body's job is to surface the why behind each frontmatter decision. The methodology-delivery Strategy paragraphs that survived from prior spec rounds stand on the canonical doc set itself.)